[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Compound Queries and Query Limitations in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firebase Firestore is a flexible, scalable database for mobile, web, and server development. While its querying capabilities are powerful, Firestore enforces strict architectural limitations on how you can combine multiple query conditions. Understanding compound queries and their limitations is crucial for designing efficient data models."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Compound Queries"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A compound query allows you to target documents that match multiple conditions. In Firestore, you can chain multiple where() methods together to filter documents by various fields. However, combining these filters introduces specific rule sets that you must follow to avoid runtime errors."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const q = query(collection(db, "products"), where("category", "==", "electronics"), where("price", "<", 500));"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Query Limitations"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Equality Operators (==): You can use multiple equality clauses on different fields without creating a composite index."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Range Operators (<, <=, >, >=): Range and inequality filters must all be applied to the exact same field."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Array-contains and In Clauses: You cannot use multiple array-contains clauses in the same query, and combining array-contains with range filters requires careful index management."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"The Orderby and Inequality Rule"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the most common pitfalls in Firestore is ordering query results. If you include an inequality filter (like <, !=, or not-in) on a field, your first orderBy clause must reference that exact same field. Violating this rule will cause Firestore to throw an invalid query exception."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Valid Query
const validQuery = query(collection(db, "users"), where("age", ">", 18), orderBy("age"));

// Invalid Query - Throws error because orderBy field does not match the inequality field
const invalidQuery = query(collection(db, "users"), where("age", ">", 18), orderBy("name"));"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Working Around Limitations with Composite Indexes"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you execute complex queries, Firestore will often output a direct link in the console to automatically generate the required composite index. Designing your data models to minimize complex multi-field range queries will keep your application performant and reduce index maintenance overhead."}]
